          "text": "     1→# use-ai High-Level Feature Requirements & Test Coverage\n     2→\n     3→This document lists user-facing capabilities in requirements format with test coverage status.\n     4→\n     5→**Legend:**\n     6→- ✅ TESTED - Feature has test coverage\n     7→- ❌ NOT TESTED - Feature exists but lacks tests\n     8→- ⚠️ PARTIAL - Feature has some test coverage but gaps remain\n     9→\n    10→---\n    11→\n    12→## Client Features\n    13→\n    14→### Tool Definition & Registration\n    15→\n    16→1. ✅ React components can expose tools (functions) to the AI using the useAI hook\n    17→2. ✅ Tools can be defined with type-safe parameters using Zod schemas\n    18→3. ✅ Tools can be defined without parameters\n    19→4. ✅ Tools can be marked as requiring confirmation before execution\n    20→   TODO: -> Use ai-sdk feature for this instead of current method\n    21→5. ✅ Tool execution errors are caught and reported back to the AI\n    22→6. ✅ Tools are automatically registered when components mount and unregistered when they unmount\n    23→\n    24→### Component State Management\n    25→\n    26→7. ✅ Components can provide their current state to the AI via the prompt option\n    27→8. ✅ The system waits for React re-renders after tool execution to capture updated state before responding\n    28→9. ✅ Components can be marked as \"invisible\" to skip render waiting (for provider-type components)\n    29→10. ✅ Multiple instances of the same component can register separate tools using unique IDs\n    30→11. ✅ Tool names are automatically namespaced when component IDs are provided\n    31→\n    32→### Chat & Conversation Management\n    33→\n    34→12. ✅ Chat history is automatically persisted to localStorage by default\n    35→13. ✅ Users can create multiple chats and switch between them\n    36→14. ✅ Chat titles are auto-generated from the first user message\n    37→15. ✅ A maximum of 20 chats are stored by default, with oldest chats auto-deleted\n    38→16. ✅ Users can delete individual chats from the history\n    39→17. ✅ Chat messages persist across page reloads\n    40→18. ✅ Full conversation context is maintained when resuming chats\n    41→19. ✅ Custom chat storage backends can be implemented via the ChatRepository interface\n    42→\n    43→### User Interface\n    44→\n    45→20. ✅ A floating button provides access to the AI chat interface\n    46→21. ✅ The floating button indicates connection status (green when connected, gray when offline)\n    47→22. ✅ The floating button shows an unread indicator when new messages arrive\n    48→23. ✅ A chat panel displays conversation history with timestamps\n    49→24. ✅ The chat panel supports multi-line input with Shift+Enter for newlines\n    50→25. ✅ The chat panel shows a \"Thinking...\" indicator while the AI processes requests\n    51→26. ❌ Empty chat displays up to 4 randomly selected suggestions from all mounted components\n    52→27. ❌ Users can click suggestions to send them as messages\n    53→28. ❌ Custom UI components can replace the default floating button\n    54→29. ❌ Custom UI components can replace the default chat panel\n    55→\n    56→### Connection & Error Handling\n    57→\n    58→30. ✅ The client automatically connects to the WebSocket server on initialization\n    59→31. ✅ The client automatically reconnects with exponential backoff on disconnect\n    60→32. ✅ Connection status is exposed to components via the useAI hook\n    61→33. ✅ The system prevents sending messages when disconnected\n    62→34. ❌ Custom error handlers can be provided via the onError callback\n    63→\n    64→### Context & System Prompts\n    65→\n    66→35. ❌ A system prompt can be configured to provide instructions to the AI\n    67→36. ❌ The useAIContext hook provides access to connection state and chat management functions\n    68→\n    69→### MCP Integration (Client-Side)\n    70→\n    71→37. ❌ Custom headers can be provided for MCP requests via the mcpHeadersProvider\n    72→38. ❌ The mcpHeadersProvider function is called on each AI invocation to get fresh headers\n    73→39. ❌ MCP header providers support exact URL matching and glob patterns\n    74→\n    75→---\n    76→\n    77→## Server Features\n    78→\n    79→### Core Architecture\n    80→\n    81→40. ✅ The server coordinates communication between client applications and AI agents using WebSocket (Socket.IO)\n    82→41. ✅ The AG-UI protocol is used for communication between client and server\n    83→42. ✅ The server maintains separate sessions for each connected client\n    84→43. ✅ The server tracks conversation history and tool calls per session\n    85→44. ✅ The server exposes a /health endpoint for Kubernetes health checks\n    86→\n    87→### Agent System\n    88→\n    89→45. ✅ Multiple AI agents can be configured (Claude, OpenAI, Google, etc. via AI SDK)\n    90→46. ✅ A default agent is specified to handle all requests\n    91→47. ✅ The AISDKAgent integrates with any AI SDK language model\n    92→48. ✅ Agents automatically handle multi-step tool execution (up to 10 steps)\n    93→49. ❌ Custom agents can be implemented by implementing the Agent interface\n    94→50. ✅ The system builds dynamic system prompts that include current application state\n    95→51. ⚠️ The system instructs the AI to ask for confirmation before calling confirmation-required tools\n    96→\n    97→### Tool Execution Coordination\n    98→\n    99→52. ✅ Tools execute on the client side where application state lives\n   100→53. ✅ The server coordinates tool calls between the AI and client\n   101→54. ✅ The server waits for tool results from the client before continuing\n   102→55. ✅ The server handles multiple sequential tool calls in a single conversation turn\n   103→56. ✅ Tool definitions are converted from client format to AI SDK format\n   104→57. ✅ The server merges client-side tools with MCP-provided tools\n   105→\n   106→### Rate Limiting\n   107→\n   108→58. ✅ Rate limiting can be configured per IP address using a sliding window algorithm\n   109→59. ✅ The maximum number of requests per window can be configured via environment variables\n   110→60. ✅ The window duration can be configured via environment variables\n   111→61. ✅ Rate limiting can be disabled by setting max requests to 0\n   112→62. ✅ Different clients have independent rate limits\n   113→63. ✅ Rate limits reset after the time window expires\n   114→64. ✅ The system returns helpful error messages with retry-after information when rate limited\n   115→\n   116→### MCP (Model Context Protocol) Integration\n   117→\n   118→65. ✅ Remote MCP servers can be specified to provide additional tools\n   119→66. ✅ MCP endpoints are configured via McpEndpointConfig array\n   120→67. ✅ Each MCP endpoint specifies a URL, optional headers, optional namespace prefix, and timeout\n   121→68. ✅ MCP tool names are prefixed with namespace to avoid conflicts\n   122→69. ✅ The server fetches MCP tool schemas on initialization using JSON-RPC 2.0\n   123→70. ✅ The server retries failed MCP endpoint initialization up to 3 times\n   124→71. ✅ MCP tools are executed via JSON-RPC 2.0 calls to the remote server\n   125→72. ✅ MCP tool execution has configurable timeout (default 30 seconds)\n   126→73. ⚠️ Custom headers can be added to MCP requests (server-wide and per-request)\n   127→74. ⚠️ Per-request headers override server-wide configured headers\n   128→75. ✅ MCP header configuration supports exact URL matching and glob patterns\n   129→76. ✅ MCP tool schemas can be periodically refreshed when configured\n   130→77. ✅ MCP endpoints are cleaned up when the server shuts down\n   131→\n   132→### Plugin Architecture\n   133→\n   134→78. ⚠️ The server supports plugins that extend functionality\n   135→79. ⚠️ Plugins can register custom message handlers for new message types\n   136→80. ⚠️ Plugins receive lifecycle hooks when clients connect and disconnect\n   137→81. ⚠️ Plugins have access to the client session for state management\n   138→82. ⚠️ The WorkflowsPlugin enables headless workflow execution\n   139→\n   140→### Observability & Logging\n   141→\n   142→83. ✅ Structured logging is available in JSON or pretty-printed format\n   143→84. ✅ Log format can be configured via LOG_FORMAT environment variable\n   144→85. ✅ The logger supports info, warn, error, and debug levels\n   145→86. ✅ The logger includes timestamps in all log entries\n   146→87. ✅ The logger redacts sensitive header values in MCP logs (unless DEBUG=1)\n   147→88. ✅ Langfuse observability can be enabled for LLM tracking and analytics\n   148→89. ✅ Langfuse integration tracks conversation sessions, tool calls, and token usage\n   149→90. ✅ Langfuse telemetry includes session metadata (sessionId, threadId, runId, ipAddress, toolCount)\n   150→91. ✅ Langfuse observability is automatically enabled when LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY and LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY are set\n   151→\n   152→### Error Handling\n   153→\n   154→92. ✅ The server emits RUN_ERROR events when agent execution fails\n   155→93. ✅ The server catches and handles AI SDK model errors\n   156→94. ✅ The server handles MCP tool execution errors and propagates them to the AI\n   157→95. ✅ The server emits helpful error messages when requested agents are not found\n   158→96. ❌ The server supports aborting in-flight agent executions via abort_run messages\n   159→\n   160→### Configuration\n   161→\n   162→97. ✅ The server port can be configured via PORT environment variable\n   163→98. ✅ The server can be initialized with multiple agents\n   164→99. ✅ API keys for different AI providers are read from environment variables\n   165→100. ✅ The server validates that at least one agent is configured on startup\n   166→101. ✅ MCP endpoint configuration can be provided via environment variables\n   167→\n   168→---\n   169→\n   170→## Workflow Features\n   171→\n   172→### Workflow Execution\n   173→\n   174→102. ❌ Headless workflows can be triggered via the useAIWorkflow hook\n   175→103. ❌ Workflows are stateless (no conversation history) unlike chat-based agents\n   176→104. ❌ Multiple workflow runners can be configured (e.g., Dify, Flowise)\n   177→105. ❌ Workflows execute via the WorkflowsPlugin on the server\n   178→106. ❌ Only one workflow can run at a time per useAIWorkflow hook instance\n   179→107. ❌ Workflow execution status is tracked (idle, running, completed, error)\n   180→\n   181→### Dify Integration\n   182→\n   183→108. ❌ Dify workflows can be integrated via the DifyWorkflowRunner\n   184→109. ❌ Dify API base URL is configurable\n   185→110. ❌ Workflow IDs map to Dify app API keys\n   186→111. ❌ The system sends requests to Dify's /workflows/run endpoint\n   187→112. ❌ The system handles Dify's Server-Sent Events (SSE) streaming responses\n   188→113. ❌ Text output from Dify workflows is streamed to the client in real-time\n   189→114. ❌ The system implements timeouts for Dify requests (100 seconds)\n   190→115. ❌ The system provides helpful error messages for Dify API failures (404, 401, 500)\n   191→\n   192→### Workflow Lifecycle & Callbacks\n   193→\n   194→116. ❌ Workflow inputs can be provided as arbitrary JSON data\n   195→117. ❌ Progress callbacks can track workflow execution (onProgress, onComplete, onError)\n   196→118. ❌ The onProgress callback is called with status updates and accumulated text\n   197→119. ❌ The onComplete callback is called with final results when workflow finishes\n   198→120. ❌ The onError callback is called with error details when workflow fails\n   199→121. ❌ Workflows emit AG-UI protocol events (RUN_STARTED, TEXT_MESSAGE_*, RUN_FINISHED, RUN_ERROR)\n   200→\n   201→### Workflow Tool Integration\n   202→\n   203→122. ❌ Workflows can call back to client-side tools defined with defineTool\n   204→123. ❌ Tools can be provided to workflows via the trigger options\n   205→124. ❌ Tool calls from workflows are tracked with names, arguments, and results\n   206→125. ❌ The onProgress callback receives updated tool call information after each execution\n   207→126. ❌ Tool execution errors are sent back to the workflow\n   208→\n   209→---\n   210→\n   211→## Protocol & Types\n   212→\n   213→### AG-UI Protocol\n   214→\n   215→127. ✅ The AG-UI protocol defines standardized event types for AI-UI communication\n   216→128. ✅ The protocol supports streaming text messages (TEXT_MESSAGE_START, _CONTENT, _END)\n   217→129. ✅ The protocol supports streaming tool calls (TOOL_CALL_START, _ARGS, _END)\n   218→130. ✅ The protocol supports run lifecycle events (RUN_STARTED, RUN_FINISHED, RUN_ERROR)\n   219→131. ✅ The protocol supports state snapshots (STATE_SNAPSHOT, MESSAGES_SNAPSHOT)\n   220→132. ❌ The protocol supports thinking messages (THINKING_TEXT_MESSAGE_*)\n   221→133. ❌ The protocol supports chunked messages (TEXT_MESSAGE_CHUNK, TOOL_CALL_CHUNK)\n   222→134. ❌ The protocol supports activity tracking (ACTIVITY_SNAPSHOT, ACTIVITY_DELTA)\n   223→135. ❌ The protocol supports step tracking (STEP_STARTED, STEP_FINISHED)\n   224→136. ❌ The protocol supports raw and custom events (RAW, CUSTOM)\n   225→137. ⚠️ All events support optional timestamps\n   226→\n   227→### Message Types\n   228→\n   229→138. ✅ The protocol supports user, assistant, system, developer, and tool message roles\n   230→139. ✅ Tool messages include tool call ID and result content\n   231→140. ✅ Assistant messages can include tool calls\n   232→141. ❌ The protocol supports activity messages for tracking activities\n   233→142. ❌ The protocol supports binary content in messages (images, files, etc.)\n   234→\n   235→### Type Safety & Exports\n   236→\n   237→143. ✅ The client exports TypeScript types for all hooks, components, and configurations\n   238→144. ✅ The server exports TypeScript types for agents, plugins, and configurations\n   239→145. ✅ The core package exports AG-UI protocol types\n   240→146. ✅ Zod is re-exported from the client package for schema definitions\n   24